steps.body:
On sorted data, two indexes that start at the two ends find a pair in one pass instead of trying every pair. Look at the sum of the two ends. If it is too small, only a bigger left value can help, so move `i` right. If it is too big, move `j` left. Each step rules out every pair with the value it leaves behind, so the walk is O(n) instead of O(n^2):

```cpp
std::vector<int> nums = {2, 7, 11, 15};
int target = 18;
int i = 0;
int j = static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1;
while (i < j) {
    int sum = nums[i] + nums[j];
    if (sum == target) {
        break;
    } else if (sum < target) {
        ++i;
    } else {
        --j;
    }
}
std::cout << i + 1 << ' ' << j + 1 << '\n';   // 2 3, the positions of 7 and 11
```

The loop also stops when `i` meets `j`, which means no pair works, so check `i < j` after the loop before you trust the answer. Keep `j` an `int` so it can't wrap around below zero. The same inward walk checks a palindrome, and it works on any data where moving one end has a predictable effect.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    std::cin >> n;
    std::vector<int> nums(n);
    for (int k = 0; k < n; ++k) {
        std::cin >> nums[k];
    }
    int q = 0;
    std::cin >> q;
    for (int t = 0; t < q; ++t) {
        int target = 0;
        std::cin >> target;
        int i = 0;
        int j = n - 1;
        while (i < j) {
            int sum = nums[i] + nums[j];
            if (sum == target) {
                break;
            } else if (sum < target) {
                ++i;
            } else {
                --j;
            }
        }
        if (i < j) {
            std::cout << target << ": " << i + 1 << ' ' << j + 1 << '\n';
        } else {
            std::cout << target << ": none" << '\n';
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

