steps.body:
An anagram of a word has the same letters the same number of times. To find every place a text holds an anagram of `p`, slide a window exactly `p.size()` letters long and keep its letter counts in a `std::vector<int>` of 26, indexed by `c - 'a'`. Two vectors compare with `==` element by element, so the window matches when its counts equal the word's:

```cpp
std::string s = "cbaebabacd";
std::string p = "abc";
std::vector<int> need(26, 0);
std::vector<int> have(26, 0);
for (char c : p) {
    ++need[c - 'a'];
}
int m = static_cast<int>(p.size());
for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(s.size()); ++i) {
    ++have[s[i] - 'a'];              // the letter coming in
    if (i >= m) {
        --have[s[i - m] - 'a'];      // the letter leaving
    }
    if (have == need) {
        std::cout << i - m + 1 << ' ';   // 0 6
    }
}
```

Each slide changes two counts, and comparing two vectors of 26 is a fixed cost, so the scan is O(n) whatever the word's length. The window starts at `i - m + 1`. A vector of 26 counts beats a map here, with no hashing, but it only works for lowercase letters, so check that the problem promises them.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    int q = 0;
    std::cin >> q;
    for (int t = 0; t < q; ++t) {
        std::string text;
        std::string word;
        std::cin >> text >> word;
        std::vector<int> need(26, 0);
        std::vector<int> have(26, 0);
        for (char c : word) {
            ++need[c - 'a'];
        }
        int m = static_cast<int>(word.size());
        std::string starts;
        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(text.size()); ++i) {
            ++have[text[i] - 'a'];
            if (i >= m) {
                --have[text[i - m] - 'a'];
            }
            if (have == need) {
                starts += ' ' + std::to_string(i - m + 1);
            }
        }
        std::cout << word << " in " << text << ':' << (starts.empty() ? " none" : starts) << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

